/*
	Exact geometric predicates over `core:math/linalg`'s vectors, with the
	same result everywhere.

	`orient2d`, `orient3d`, `incircle`, and `insphere` return the exact sign of
	their determinant for `f32` and `f64` coordinates, as if computed in real
	arithmetic, so code built on them cannot contradict itself: three points
	are collinear or not, whichever order they are tested in. Each first
	computes the determinant in floating point with an error bound, and only a
	result within the bound, near a degenerate configuration, is recomputed
	exactly. Integers get the plain determinant, exact while it does not
	overflow, and other number types its sign as computed.

	Every result is the same on every machine, CPU level, and optimization
	level, and at compile time.
*/
package geometry;

import "base:interfaces";
import "core:math/linalg";

/*
	The sign of a predicate's determinant. What `.Positive` means is stated on
	each predicate.
*/
Sign :: enum { Negative, Zero, Positive }

/*
	Which side of the line through `a` and `b` the point `c` is on:
	`.Positive` when `a`, `b`, `c` turn counterclockwise, `.Negative` when
	clockwise, and `.Zero` when they are collinear. It is the sign of
	`(a.x - c.x)*(b.y - c.y) - (a.y - c.y)*(b.x - c.x)`.

	Exact for `f32` over its whole finite range, for `f64` coordinates whose
	magnitudes are 0 or between `2^-450` and `2^450`, and for integers whose
	products do not overflow, which panics. Other types get the sign of that
	formula in `T`. A NaN or infinite coordinate gives a sign that is the same
	everywhere but means nothing.
*/
orient2d :: proc(a, b, c: linalg.Vector2($T)) -> Sign where interfaces.Numeric(T), interfaces.Ordered(T) ---;

/*
	Which side of the plane through `a`, `b`, and `c` the point `d` is on:
	`.Positive` when `d` is below it, where `a`, `b`, `c` appear
	counterclockwise seen from above, `.Negative` when above, and `.Zero` when
	the four are coplanar. It is the sign of the determinant of the rows
	`a - d`, `b - d`, and `c - d`.

	Exact for `f32` over its whole finite range, for `f64` coordinates whose
	magnitudes are 0 or between `2^-280` and `2^300`, and for integers whose
	products do not overflow; otherwise as for `orient2d`.
*/
orient3d :: proc(a, b, c, d: linalg.Vector3($T)) -> Sign where interfaces.Numeric(T), interfaces.Ordered(T) ---;

/*
	Whether `d` is inside the circle through `a`, `b`, and `c`, given in
	counterclockwise order: `.Positive` inside, `.Negative` outside, and
	`.Zero` on it; the signs swap for clockwise `a`, `b`, `c`. It is the sign
	of the determinant of the rows `(p.x - d.x, p.y - d.y, |p - d|^2)` for `p`
	in `a`, `b`, `c`.

	Exact for `f32` over its whole finite range, for `f64` coordinates whose
	magnitudes are 0 or between `2^-200` and `2^220`, and for integers whose
	products do not overflow; otherwise as for `orient2d`.
*/
incircle :: proc(a, b, c, d: linalg.Vector2($T)) -> Sign where interfaces.Numeric(T), interfaces.Ordered(T) ---;

/*
	Whether `e` is inside the sphere through `a`, `b`, `c`, and `d`, given so
	that `orient3d(a, b, c, d)` is `.Positive`: `.Positive` inside,
	`.Negative` outside, and `.Zero` on it; the signs swap for the other
	orientation. It is the sign of the determinant of the rows
	`(p.x - e.x, p.y - e.y, p.z - e.z, |p - e|^2)` for `p` in `a`, `b`, `c`,
	`d`.

	Exact for `f32` over its whole finite range, for `f64` coordinates whose
	magnitudes are 0 or between `2^-150` and `2^180`, and for integers whose
	products do not overflow; otherwise as for `orient2d`. Its exact path, met
	only near a degenerate configuration, uses about 120 KB of stack.
*/
insphere :: proc(a, b, c, d, e: linalg.Vector3($T)) -> Sign where interfaces.Numeric(T), interfaces.Ordered(T) ---;
